(4850611, 701687, 66384, '<td valign="top">What a lovely shot. The pattern of the cut crystal makes an excellent composition. I like the tilt and the DOF. The thermometer is a nice sharp contrast to the icy blue coldness of the rest of the image.</td>', 'What a lovely shot. The pattern of the cut crystal makes an excellent composition. I like the tilt and the DOF. The thermometer is a nice sharp contrast to the icy blue coldness of the rest of the image.', '2008-07-30 01:49:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(4850612, 702173, 73981, '<td valign="top">Good job...now how do you get access to liquid Nitrogen?</td>', 'Good job...now how do you get access to liquid Nitrogen?', '2008-07-30 01:49:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(4850648, 702064, 37430, '<td valign="top">SUPERB! Thats what it is! Very well conveys cold at multiple levels. Those goose pimples really tell how cold it is :) The lighting, contours and the processing compliment each other very well. Its an art!</td>', 'SUPERB! Thats what it is! Very well conveys cold at multiple levels. Those goose pimples really tell how cold it is :) The lighting, contours and the processing compliment each other very well. Its an art!', '2008-07-30 02:00:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(4850711, 702173, 90495, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by heavyj:</b></div><hr/><i> Good job...now how do you get access to liquid Nitrogen? </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Ahh yes, the liquid nitrogen. It wasn''t LN2. It was a Dust Off can turned upside down. The gases used to pressurize the can are very cold and that''s what spurts out if turned upside down and used. I sprayed this liquid/gas stuff over the flower and waited until it froze up and during that time this shot was taken because of the beautiful trails of water vapor. I was amazed at the delicacy of the misty trails and wanted that to show up, couldn''t care about the flower any more at that point.<br/> <br/>Maybe a physicist can explain what exactly is going on here.<br/> <br/>Thanks for the comps and comments everyone. :D</td>', 'Originally posted by heavyj: Good job...now how do you get access to liquid Nitrogen?   Ahh yes, the liquid nitrogen. It wasn''t LN2. It was a Dust Off can turned upside down. The gases used to pressurize the can are very cold and that''s what spurts out if turned upside down and used. I sprayed this liquid/gas stuff over the flower and waited until it froze up and during that time this shot was taken because of the beautiful trails of water vapor. I was amazed at the delicacy of the misty trails and wanted that to show up, couldn''t care about the flower any more at that point. Maybe a physicist can explain what exactly is going on here. Thanks for the comps and comments everyone. :D', '2008-07-30 02:20:28.000000', NULL, 0),
